@page "/async"

<PageTitle>Async rules</PageTitle>
<h1>Async rules</h1>
<p>Type "admin" and pause: each keystroke starts the check immediately (the delay simulates a server call — 600 ms by default, adjustable below), shows a pending state, and a new keystroke cancels the in-flight check.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "Handle.cs", "AsyncRules.razor", "AsyncRules.razor.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Username</strong> — checked for uniqueness asynchronously while you type (a delay stands in for the server — 600&nbsp;ms by default, adjustable below). Taken usernames: <code>admin</code>, <code>root</code>, <code>formidable</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Display name</strong> — checked for uniqueness the same way, independently of Username. Taken display names: <code>Administrator</code>, <code>Root User</code>, <code>Formidable</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Username</strong> — also required at submit.</li>
            <li>Editing after a submit also schedules a background refresh (300&nbsp;ms) that reconciles the whole form — but the pending indicator stays on the field you edited, before and after submit alike.</li>
            <li>After a submit, one edit runs the check twice: the Submit profile includes the draft rules, so the live pass runs it and the refresh runs it again. The two answer different channels — the live pass answers the field being edited, the refresh keeps the submit's own messages current — so neither can stand in for the other.</li>
            <li><strong>LiveDebounce</strong> batches keystrokes: tick the checkbox below and a burst of typing starts one check after you pause, instead of one per keystroke.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Type <code>admin</code> and pause — a spinner and "checking…" appear on that field only, then the taken message lands.</li>
            <li>Type quickly — each keystroke cancels the in-flight check; only the final value gets a verdict.</li>
            <li>Type in Display name — its indicator runs independently; the pending state is scoped to the field being edited.</li>
            <li>Clear Username and submit — the required rule blocks.</li>
            <li>Drag the delay to 2000 ms and type — the pending state lingers long enough to watch supersession cancel a stale check mid-flight.</li>
            <li>Submit, then type with the delay still high — the check lands, and a second one starts as the refresh comes due.</li>
            <li>Tick <em>Debounce live checks</em>, then type <code>formidable</code> fast — no "checking…" flashes mid-keystroke. Because you already submitted in the step above, pausing now starts two passes: the 300&nbsp;ms refresh and the batched 400&nbsp;ms live pass, refresh first.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<p>
    <label>Simulated delay
        <input type="range" min="0" max="2000" step="100" @bind="DelayMs" @bind:event="oninput" />
    </label>
    @HandleValidator.SimulatedDelayMs ms
</p>

<p>
    <label><input type="checkbox" @bind="LiveDebounceEnabled" /> Debounce live checks (batch fast typing into one pass)</label>
</p>

<FormidableForm Model="_handle" Options="_options" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid">
    <FormidableSummary />

    <FormidableField For="() => _handle.Username" Context="field">
        <div class="field">
            <label>Username <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_handle.Username" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" /></label>
            @if (field.State.IsValidating)
            {
                <em role="status">checking…</em>
            }
        </div>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _handle.Username" />
    </FormidableField>

    <FormidableField For="() => _handle.DisplayName" Context="field">
        <div class="field">
            <label>Display name <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_handle.DisplayName" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" /></label>
            @if (field.State.IsValidating)
            {
                <em role="status">checking…</em>
            }
        </div>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _handle.DisplayName" />
    </FormidableField>

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>

<p><small>The pending flag is scoped to the field being edited during a live pass — type in Username or Display name and only that field's "checking…" indicator shows, not both.</small></p>
